>has a probability wavefunction. If the particle decays, then (somehow) the gas
>in the bottle is released killing the cat. HOWEVER, the lid of the box has a
>tripwire on it - opening the box will also release the gas and kill the cat.
>The point is this: If you open the box, the cat is always dead. But you don't
>know whether it died from the particle decaying or from opening the box.


Hmmm, I always thought there were two points:
	
	1) As everyone said, the cat is in a part alive/part dead
	state.  Mathematically, it's described as a set of statistically
	weighted possibilities.  This is strange.  The cat is neither
	dead nor alive, but a set of stats.

	2) The values of that set of stats depend critically on when
	the box is opened, which is why *I* thought a readioactive
	decay was used.  You can't look at the cat without drastically
	changing it...



